ADOPT: THREE STAGED SESSIONS + AN OVERLAY RESOLVER. Concretely, in /Users/macbookpro/Documents/AINCORE-Blockchain/core/vm_move/src/lib.rs.

AINCORE is better positioned than Aptos for this because `AINCOREStorage` (lib.rs:20-110) implements only two trait methods over flat string keys, and `changeset_to_kv` (lib.rs:614-650) already emits exactly those keys as `Vec<(String, Option<String>)>`. None of Aptos's complexity (aggregators, delayed fields, resource groups, ChangeSetConfigs) applies.

STEP 1 — the overlay resolver (AINCORE's `ExecutorViewWithChangeSet`, ~40 lines, new file `core/vm_move/src/overlay.rs`):

    pub struct OverlayStorage<'a> {
        base: &'a AINCOREStorage,
        // key -> Some(hex value) = write, None = delete. Same key space as changeset_to_kv.
        overlay: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Option<String>>,
    }
    impl ResourceResolver for OverlayStorage<'_> {
        fn get_resource(&self, addr, typ) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>, _> {
            let key = format!("resource_{}_{}", addr, typ);
            match self.overlay.get(&key) {
                Some(Some(hex)) => Ok(Some(hex::decode(hex)?)),
                Some(None)      => Ok(None),      // CRITICAL: a staged delete is a HIT, not a miss
                None            => self.base.get_resource(addr, typ),
            }
        }
    }
    // identical shape for ModuleResolver::get_module with key `module_{addr}_{name}`

STEP 2 — restructure `execute_transaction_actions` (lib.rs:469-556) into three stages sharing ONE gas meter. Replace the current single `let mut session = self.vm.new_session(&self.storage);` + loop + unconditional `session.finish()`.

    pub fn execute_transaction_actions(&self, actions, sender, gas_limit) -> ExecutionResult {
        let mut gas_meter = AINCOREGasMeter::new(gas_limit);   // ONE meter, all stages
        let mut staged: BTreeMap<String, Option<String>> = BTreeMap::new();
        let (pre, user): (Vec<_>, Vec<_>) = actions.into_iter().partition(|(_,ms,_)| *ms);

        // --- STAGE 1: PROLOGUE (must_succeed actions: coin::deduct_gas, nonce bump) ---
        // Runs against the raw DB. Failure => DISCARD THE WHOLE TX (Aptos unwrap_or_discard!).
        {
            let mut s = self.vm.new_session(&self.storage);
            for (action, _, auth) in pre {
                run_action(&mut s, action, auth, sender, &mut gas_meter)?;  // `?` = discard tx
            }
            let (cs, _ev) = s.finish()?;
            squash(&mut staged, self.changeset_to_kv(cs)?);
        }
        self.vm.flush_loader_cache_if_invalidated();   // only needed if stage 1 can publish

        // --- STAGE 2: USER PAYLOAD (may abort) ---
        let mut status = ExecutionStatus::success();
        {
            let overlay = OverlayStorage::new(&self.storage, staged.clone());
            let mut s = self.vm.new_session(&overlay);
            let mut ok = true;
            for (action, _, auth) in user {
                if let Err(e) = run_action(&mut s, action, auth, sender, &mut gas_meter) {
                    status = ExecutionStatus::aborted(e.to_string());
                    ok = false;
                    break;                       // stop at first abort
                }
            }
            if ok {
                let (cs, _ev) = s.finish()?;
                squash(&mut staged, self.changeset_to_kv(cs)?);
            } else {
                drop(s);                         // <-- THE ROLLBACK. never call finish().
            }
            // `overlay` dies here; `staged` is owned and outlives it. No self-referential struct
            // needed (this is why AINCORE does not need Aptos's ouroboros-style RespawnedSession).
        }

        // --- STAGE 3: EPILOGUE (gas true-up / refund). Runs over `staged`, UNMETERED. ---
        {
            let overlay = OverlayStorage::new(&self.storage, staged.clone());
            let mut s = self.vm.new_session(&overlay);
            let mut unmetered = AINCOREGasMeter::unmetered();
            // e.g. coin::refund_gas(sender, (gas_limit - gas_meter.gas_used()) * price)
            run_epilogue(&mut s, sender, &gas_meter, &mut unmetered)?;
            let (cs, _ev) = s.finish()?;
            squash(&mut staged, self.changeset_to_kv(cs)?);
        }

        Ok((gas_meter.gas_used(), staged.into_iter().collect(), status))
    }

`squash` is plain per-key last-write-wins into the BTreeMap (`for (k,v) in new { staged.insert(k,v); }`). That is correct here — unlike Aptos, AINCORE's ops are whole-value overwrites or deletes, so New/Modify/Delete collapse trivially. BTreeMap ordering makes the emitted Vec deterministic; do NOT re-sort by tx_hash (that is the BLOCKER-2 bug).

STEP 3 — delete the false comment and the executor's ability to get it wrong. Remove `println!("⚠️ Payload execution failed (state reverted)")` at lib.rs:544 (it was a lie; after this change it is true and the println is noise). Then in /Users/macbookpro/Documents/AINCORE-Blockchain/core/executor/src/lib.rs the `absorb_vm_result` macro (:2739-2757) no longer needs to gate on `status.success` — the VM now returns an already-correct write set on both paths, exactly as Aptos's `VMOutput` from `failed_transaction_cleanup` is authoritative and the adapter never sees the discarded set. Keep the status check anyway as defence in depth, but the ordering bug (stage-before-check) becomes harmless rather than fatal. This is the important design property: make the wrong thing unrepresentable at the VM boundary instead of relying on every caller to filter.

STEP 4 — gas model. Move to the Aptos/Diem prologue-deposit model to match the new structure: stage 1's `coin::deduct_gas` withdraws `gas_limit * gas_price` (the max), stage 3 deposits `(gas_limit - gas_used) * gas_price` back. Today's flat up-front deduct also works and needs no stage 3, but then an abort at instruction 1 costs the same as one at instruction 1,000,000 — acceptable for launch, but the stage-3 slot should exist from day one because retrofitting it later reopens the same session-mixing question.

INTERACTION WITH THE OTHER BLOCKERS: stage 1 and stage 3 write real keys (the sender's CoinStore, and any fee/refund destination). Those keys MUST appear in `analyze_tx`'s declared dependency set for every transaction type, or the BLOCKER-2 fix is incomplete — splitting sessions ADDS write keys to transactions that previously appeared to touch only their module's resources. Fix BLOCKER-2's conflict tokens with the staged writes already in mind, ideally by deriving deps from the actual key set rather than hand-written per-module lists.

Five concrete changes, ordered by how much fork risk each removes. All of them are "make the decision a function of the certified DAG, never of what this node happens to hold."

--- P1. DELETE the round-robin leader fallback (fixes ordering.rs:503-536, and get_leader_with_fallback's `attempt` seed at :~730). ---
Current: `for attempt in 0..max_attempts { let leader_id = get_leader_with_fallback(anchor_round, validators, attempt); if <leader's vertex is in MY dag> { break } }` — and the seed is `round + vdf_seed + attempt`, so each attempt elects a *genuinely different* validator. Node A holding L1's vertex commits anchor L1; node B not holding it commits anchor L2. Both then set `finalized_round = anchor_round` and neither ever revisits the round. That is a permanent, silent fork.
Replace with the narwhal shape:

    let leader_id = self.get_leader(anchor_round, validators);          // attempt param GONE
    let anchor_vertex_hash = match round_index.get(&anchor_round)
        .and_then(|hs| hs.iter().find(|h| dag.get(*h).is_some_and(|v| v.author == leader_id)))
    {
        Some(h) => h,
        None => return None,   // UNDECIDED — wait, do not substitute, do not advance finalized_round
    };

Liveness is not lost: it is recovered by P2, not by picking a different leader.

--- P2. ADD skip + retroactive back-fill; make `finalized_round` advance only over DECIDED rounds (fixes the permanent-gap bug in ordering.rs:487-491 + :592-596). ---
Today an anchor round that fails the support check is skipped forever with no record and no later reconsideration — so two nodes can differ in the *set* of committed vertices, not merely the order. Adopt Bullshark Alg.5 `commit_leader` / narwhal `order_leaders`:

    // On a successful commit of anchor A at round R, walk BACKWARD before emitting anything.
    let mut to_commit = vec![A.clone()];
    let mut cursor = A;
    let mut r = R.saturating_sub(2);
    while r > self.finalized_round {
        if let Some(prev) = self.leader_vertex_at(r, validators, dag, round_index) {
            if self.strong_path(&cursor, &prev, dag, round_index) {   // path over `parents` only
                to_commit.push(prev.clone());
                cursor = prev;
            }
            // else: DETERMINISTIC SKIP of round r (no path from a committed anchor)
        }
        r = r.saturating_sub(2);
    }
    to_commit.reverse();  // oldest first
    for leader in to_commit { sequence.extend(self.find_causal_history(&leader, dag)); }

Then `self.finalized_round = R` is correct, because every round in (old_finalized, R] has now been explicitly decided commit-or-skip by a rule that reads only A's causal history — identical on every node. Keep the existing >2/3 stake support check at :572 as the *direct* rule; the back-fill is the *indirect* rule that repairs nodes that missed it.
(Optionally add Mysticeti's direct-skip: 2f+1 stake of round R+1 vertices whose `parents` contain NO vertex authored by the leader => Skip immediately, which restores latency after a genuinely dead leader without any fallback election.)

--- P3. MAKE find_causal_history DEFER instead of silently skipping (fixes ordering.rs:755-772). ---
Current inner loop is `if let Some(vertex) = dag.get(&hash) { history.push(..); ... }` with no else — a missing parent is dropped and the block is emitted anyway. Change the signature to fallible and treat a gap as "not yet committable":

    fn find_causal_history(&self, anchor: &str, dag: &HashMap<String, Vertex>)
        -> Result<Vec<String>, Vec<String>>   // Err = the missing parent hashes
    {
        ...
        match dag.get(&hash) {
            Some(v) => { history.push(hash.clone()); for p in &v.parents {
                            if p != "genesis" && !self.committed_set.contains(p) { stack.push(p.clone()) } } }
            None => missing.push(hash.clone()),
        }
        if !missing.is_empty() { return Err(missing); }
        ...
    }

`try_commit` on `Err(missing)` must return `None` WITHOUT touching `committed_rounds` / `finalized_round`, and hand `missing` to a fetcher (P4). Also drop the `(Some,None)/(None,Some)` arms of the sort comparator at :783-789 — once completeness is enforced they are unreachable, and their existence is what makes the current silent-skip look intentional. Keep the sort key but make it total and explicit like Mysticeti: `round ASC, then author ASC, then hash ASC` (author before hash removes any dependence on hash-preimage grinding by a proposer choosing among its own equivocations).

--- P4. GATE add_vertex ON PARENT PRESENCE + ADD A VERTEX FETCHER (fixes dag.rs:332 and the root cause of everything above). ---
`add_vertex` currently never inspects `vertex.parents` at all (grep confirms: `parents` appears in dag.rs only inside try_create_vertex). Mirror Bullshark Alg.2 L51-59 / narwhal Core::process_certificate:

    // in add_vertex, AFTER signature/validator-set/equivocation checks,
    // BEFORE inserting into self.dag / self.round_index / persisting:
    if vertex.round > 1 {
        let missing: Vec<String> = { let dag = self.dag.lock()...;
            vertex.parents.iter().filter(|p| *p != "genesis" && !dag.contains_key(*p)).cloned().collect() };
        if !missing.is_empty() {
            self.pending_vertices.insert(vertex.hash.clone(), (vertex.clone(), missing.iter().cloned().collect()));
            for m in &missing { self.missing_index.entry(m.clone()).or_default().insert(vertex.hash.clone()); }
            self.p2p_tx.try_send(format!("REQ_VERTEX:{}", missing.join(","))).ok();
            return;   // SUSPENDED — not stored, not counted, not shown to ordering
        }
    }
    // ... on successful insert, drain pending_vertices children of this hash (try_unsuspend_children_blocks)

Serving side goes in `chain_sync::handle_message` (per the repo's dual-path memory note), not in common/network. Bound `pending_vertices` by round window (reject anything <= finalized_round - 10, same window as the existing pruner) so it cannot be memory-griefed.
Also require the parent-stake quorum on INBOUND vertices, mirroring Narwhal block-validity condition 3: a round-r vertex must reference parents at r-1 whose distinct authors clear >2/3 stake. AINCORE already has exactly this predicate (`qc::stake_quorum_met`) but applies it only to its OWN vertex at dag.rs:360-379.

--- P5. DERIVE THE ROUND FAST-FORWARD FROM THE LOCAL QUORUM, NOT FROM ONE REMOTE VERTEX (fixes dag.rs:816-821). ---
Replace `if vertex.author != self.node_id && vertex.round > self.current_round { self.current_round = vertex.round + 1; }` with the Bullshark Alg.2 L53-55 / ThresholdClock form — jump only to a round for which the parent quorum is ALREADY held locally:

    // after the vertex is actually inserted (i.e. it passed P4's completeness gate):
    let target = vertex.round;                  // NOT vertex.round + 1
    if vertex.author != self.node_id && target > self.current_round {
        let stake_at_target = self.distinct_author_stake_at_round(target, &validators);
        if crate::qc::stake_quorum_met(stake_at_target, total_stake) {
            self.current_round = target + 1;    // we hold a quorum AT target, so target+1 is buildable
            let _ = self.storage.put("latest_proposed_round", &self.current_round.to_string());
        } else if target > self.current_round {
            self.current_round = target;        // at most: catch up TO the observed round, never past it
            let _ = self.storage.put(...);
        }
    }

The key property (Mysticeti's in-source justification) is that `current_round = X` must imply "I hold a parent quorum at X-1". With P4 in place that becomes an enforceable invariant; without P4 it is unenforceable, which is why P4 and P5 must land together. MAX_ROUND_JUMP / ABSOLUTE_ROUND_CEILING become redundant safety nets rather than the primary defence.

--- P6 (the fold-in HIGH, blockchain/src/lib.rs:56). ---
`new_with_roots` calls `SystemTime::now()` and folds it into the header hash. Every reference impl derives the commit timestamp from the DAG, not the wall clock: Mysticeti computes the sub-dag timestamp as a stake-weighted median over the leader's parent round (`linearizer.rs` `timestamps.sort_by_key(|(ts,_)| *ts)` + quorum walk) and additionally clamps it monotonic with `timestamp_ms.max(last_commit_timestamp_ms)`. Apply the same here: pass the anchor vertex's already-signed `timestamp` (or the stake-weighted median of the anchor's parents' timestamps) into `new_with_roots` as an explicit argument, clamped to be >= the previous block's timestamp. Every node then derives an identical header hash. Also move sync/src/lib.rs:974 so `verify_execution_roots` runs BEFORE the commit, not after it.

changes.detail:
Body becomes: `let mut gas_meter = AINCOREGasMeter::new(gas_limit);` (ONE meter, all stages) + `let mut staged: BTreeMap<String, Option<String>> = BTreeMap::new();` + `let mut status = ExecutionStatus::success();`.

SPLIT (do NOT `partition` — that silently reorders): `let split = actions.iter().position(|(_, ms, _)| !*ms).unwrap_or(actions.len()); let user = actions.split_off(split); let prologue = actions;` then `if user.iter().any(|(_, ms, _)| *ms) { anyhow::bail!("must_succeed action ordered after a fallible action") }`. Every live call site already has the must_succeed pre-actions as a leading prefix (executor :2812/:2914 clone `pre_actions` then push the payload; :1148 passes a single must_succeed action).

STAGE 1 (prologue, all-or-nothing): `let mut s = self.vm.new_session(&self.storage);` run each action with `?` so a failure returns Err and the executor discards the ENTIRE tx (never a partial prologue — that would recreate B1 one level up); then `let (cs, _ev) = s.finish()?; squash(&mut staged, self.changeset_to_kv(cs)?);`.

STAGE 2 (user payload, may abort) — scoped so the overlay's borrow of `staged` ends before the squash (avoids the clone the naive version needs):
```
let kv = {
    let overlay = OverlayStorage::new(&self.storage, &staged);
    let mut s = self.vm.new_session(&overlay);
    let mut ok = true;
    for (action, _, auth) in user {
        if let Err(e) = Self::run_action(&mut s, action, auth, sender, &mut gas_meter) {
            status = ExecutionStatus::aborted(e.to_string());
            ok = false;
            break;                 // ONE abort discards the WHOLE user stage
        }
    }
    if ok { let (cs, _ev) = s.finish()?; Some(self.changeset_to_kv(cs)?) }
    else  { drop(s); None }        // <-- THE ROLLBACK: finish() is never called
};
if let Some(kv) = kv { squash(&mut staged, kv); }
```
After any stage that ran a `MoveAction::PublishModule`, call `self.vm.flush_loader_cache_if_invalidated()` (verified at move-vm/runtime/src/move_vm.rs:102) — the Loader cache lives on the MoveVM, not the Session, and is the one piece of state layering does NOT isolate.

STAGE 3: reserved, currently zero actions (gas is a flat up-front burn, see below). Leave a documented `run_epilogue` seam; when wired it MUST use an unmetered meter, else an abort that consumed the full limit makes the fee write itself OOG and aborts become free again.

Return `Ok((gas_meter.gas_used(), staged.into_iter().collect(), status))` — BTreeMap iteration order is deterministic; do NOT re-sort by tx_hash at this layer (that is the B2 bug).
